  "explanation": "pandas resample on a datetime index defaults to label='left' and closed='left', so the bar stamped 2024-01-01 covering the calendar window [01-01, 01-03) is labelled with its FIRST day but its value comes from .last() — the close of the LAST trading day inside that window. The 3-day bar's close is therefore a future observation relative to the timestamp it carries. Reindexing that left-labelled, forward-filled series onto the daily index then propagates each 3-day close backwards onto days 1 and 2 of its own window, so the filter `htf_close > htf_ma` evaluated on day t already contains the close of day t+1 (and the 10-period rolling mean is built from further future closes). The signal `daily_htf_bull & (daily_rsi == 1)` is thus computed from information not available at the decision time — classic lookahead. A second consequence of the same call: '3D' resamples on calendar days, not trading days, so windows drift against the session calendar and a 3-calendar-day bin can contain 1, 2 or 3 trading days (e.g. a Friday–Monday window), making the '3-day bar' and its 10-period MA inconsistently specified. The correct construction is to resample on trading-day frequency ('3B' or a session-count groupby) and to label/shift the higher-timeframe series so only completed bars are mapped forward (e.g. closed='right', label='right', then ffill). Note the additional NaN semantics: rolling(10).mean() is NaN for the first nine bars, and .astype(int) with .fillna(0) silently codes 'unknown trend' as bearish; this is conservative and does not by itself create a wrong long entry, so it is secondary to the leakage above."
}

