  "explanation": "Buy sizing includes slippage and commission, while sell sizing uses the raw open price. Neither branch solves for the target weight against post-transaction equity. Starting fully invested with N shares and zero cash, signal=0.5 sells floor(N/2) shares: rounding leaves excess exposure when N is odd, and selling costs reduce equity, leaving the remaining position above 50% even when N is even. Thus the down-scaling is not cost-adjusted symmetrically with buying; truncation under-sells rather than oversells. The function returns only an order quantity, so the exact realized weight depends on the downstream execution model. For positive equity and an existing long position, signal<0 requests a negative target but min(pos, desired_sell) limits the sale to the existing position, liquidating rather than opening a short. When flat, a negative signal produces no trade. This is a long-only constraint incompatible with interpreting negative signals as short target weights."
}

